// Formative practice quiz for loading-data-with-useeffect. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/effects-routing-and-rendering/loading-data-with-useeffect

::loading-data-with-useeffect o1 - where fetching goes::A component must load a list when it appears on screen. Where does the fetch call belong?{
=Inside useEffect, storing the result with a state setter#Correct. An Effect runs after render and is meant for synchronizing with something outside React.
~Directly in the component body during render#Not quite. Rendering must stay pure, and this would fetch on every render.
~In the JSX, inside curly braces#Not quite. That is still during render, and a promise cannot be rendered.
~In package.json#Not quite. package.json lists dependencies and scripts, and runs no page code.
}

::loading-data-with-useeffect o2 - empty array meaning::What does an empty dependency array tell React about an Effect?{
=Run it after the first mount only#Correct. With no dependencies listed, nothing can trigger a re-run.
~Never run it#Not quite. It still runs once, after the first mount.
~Run it after every render#Not quite. That is what leaving the array out entirely does.
~Run it before the first render#Not quite. Effects run after render.
}

::loading-data-with-useeffect o2 - wrong array::An Effect reads a userId prop but has an empty dependency array. userId changes. What does the screen show?{
=Data for the old user, because the Effect does not re-run#Correct. The Effect read a value it did not list, so it never re-runs.
~Data for the new user, because React tracks props automatically#Not quite. React only compares the values listed in the array.
~A compile error about the missing value#Not quite. It compiles. A lint rule can warn you, but the code still runs.
~Nothing, because the component unmounts#Not quite. A prop change does not unmount the component.
}

::loading-data-with-useeffect o3 - stale response::You switch quickly from item 1 to item 2, and the slow response for item 1 arrives last. What prevents it from overwriting the screen?{
=A cleanup function that ignores or aborts the earlier request#Correct. React runs cleanup before the next Effect run, so the old request can be ignored or aborted.
~Adding a second useState#Not quite. More state does not stop the old response from landing.
~Removing the dependency array#Not quite. That adds more requests and does not handle ordering.
~Nothing, because responses always arrive in order#Not quite. Network responses can arrive in any order.
}

::loading-data-with-useeffect o3 - strict mode::Why does a component run its Effect twice on mount in development?{
=Strict Mode remounts on purpose to expose a missing cleanup#Correct. This development-only check runs setup, cleanup and setup again.
~Because the dependency array is wrong#Not quite. A correct array still gives this development-only double run.
~Because production needs two requests#Not quite. This does not happen in production.
~Because Vite reloads the page twice#Not quite. Vite is not what causes this. It comes from React Strict Mode.
}
